{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Except the bread that he ate.</b> Rashi explained: this is his wife, as it is written ״וְלֹא חָשַׂךְ מִמֶּנִּי מְאוּמָה כִּי אִם אוֹתָךְ בַּאֲשֶׁר אַתְּ אִשְׁתּוֹ״ (Genesis 39:9). And he called his wife by the designation 'bread,' for just as bread satisfies every living thing more than any other food, so too permitted relations satisfy that organ — as opposed to forbidden relations, which do not satisfy. As our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Sukkah 52b): ״אֵבָר קָטָן, מַשְׂבִּיעוֹ רָעֵב״ — a small organ, the hungry one satisfies it; and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Sanhedrin 75a) that the taste of relations was taken away and given to transgressors, for his inclination has no power except in a forbidden matter. And therefore our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Sotah 4a): ״כָּל הַבָּא עַל אִשָּׁה זוֹנָה לְסוֹף מְבַקֵּשׁ כִּכַּר לֶחֶם וְאֵינוֹ מוֹצֵא״ — whoever lies with a forbidden woman, in the end seeks a loaf of bread and does not find it, as it is said (Proverbs 6:26) ״כִּי בְעַד אִשָּׁה זוֹנָה עַד כִּכַּר לָחֶם״. And his punishment is measure for measure: because he abandoned his wife, who is called 'bread,' and cleaved to a forbidden woman who is not his bread, therefore his bread will be lacking to him." "And there are those who say, this bread is literal bread, for it was an abomination to the Egyptians to eat bread with the Hebrews. And according to this we can explain what is said ״וְלֹא יָדַע אִתּוֹ מְאוּמָה״ — which refers to Joseph: for even though he left everything he had in Joseph's hand and Joseph could have eaten from every kind of delicacy, and who would rebuke him, for everything was in his hand — nevertheless, Joseph did not know with him anything: he did not enjoy any morsel of his master's bread except the bread that he ate — bread with salt alone he ate, according to the way of Torah; and even so, ״וַיְהִי יוֹסֵף יְפֵה תֹאַר וִיפֵה מַרְאֶה״ — as at first, and the glow of his countenance did not change, as happened to Daniel and his companions (Daniel 1). And with this is resolved what they ask: is it only now that he became beautiful of form and appearance? Was he not so already from the beginning? Rather, for emphasis it states here that he did not change, because he did not wish to feast on his master's morsel."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:34.435375543Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}